" (https://assets.cambridge.org/97810092/30056/frontmatter/9781009230056_frontmatter.pdf)\nciteturn834view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 14; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://assets.cambridge.org/97810092/30056/frontmatter/9781009230056_frontmatter.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 382\nL0@P0: Cambridge University Press & Assessment\nL1@P0: 978-1-009-23005-6 — Fourier Analysis\nL2@P0: T. W. Körner , Foreword by Terence Tao\nL3@P0: Frontmatter\nL4@P0: More Information\nL5@P0: © in this web service Cambridge University Press & Assessment www.cambridge.org\nL6@P0: FOUR IER ANALYS IS\nL7@P0: Fourier analysis is a subject that was born in physics but grew up in mathematics. Now it is part\nL8@P0: of the standard repertoire for mathematicians, physicists and engineers. This diversity of interest\nL9@P0: is often overlooked, but in this much-loved book, Tom Körner provides a shop window for some\nL10@P0: of the ideas, techniques and elegant results of Fourier analysis, and for their applications. These\nL11@P0: range from number theory, numerical analysis, control theory and statistics, to earth science,\nL12@P0: astronomy and electrical engineering. The prerequisites are few (a reader with knowledge of\nL13@P0: second- or third-year undergraduate mathematics should have no difficulty following the text),\nL14@P0: and the style is lively and entertaining.\nL15@P0: This edition of Körner’s 1989 text includes a foreword written by Professor Terence Tao\nL16@P0: introducing it to a new generation of fans.\nL17@P0: T. W. Körner is Emeritus Professor of Fourier Analysis at the University of Cambridge. His\nL18@P0: other books include The Pleasures of Counting (Cambridge, 1996) and Where Do Numbers Come\nL19@P0-1: From? (Cambridge, 2019).Cambridge University Press & Assessment\nL20@P1: 978-1-009-23005-6 — Fourier Analysis\nL21@P1: T. W. Körner , Foreword by Terence Tao\nL22@P1: Frontmatter\nL23@P1: More Information\nL24@P1: © in this web service Cambridge University Press & Assessment www.cambridge.org\nL25@P1: CAMBRIDGE\nL26@P1: MATHEMATICAL LIBRARY\nL27@P1: Cambridge University Press has a long and honourable history of publishing in mathematics\nL28@P1: and counts many classics of the mathematical literature within its list. Some of these titles\nL29@P1: have been out of print for many years now and yet the methods which they espouse are still\nL30@P1: of considerable relevance today.\nL31@P1: The Cambridge Mathematical Library provides an inexpensive edition of these titles in\nL32@P1: a durable paperback format and at a price that will make the books attractive to individuals\nL33@P1: wishing to add them to their own personal libraries. Certain volumes in the series have a\nL34@P1: foreword, written by a leading expert in the subject, which places the title in its historical\nL35@P1: and mathematical context.\nL36@P1: A complete list of books in the series can be found at www.cambridge.org/mathematics.\nL37@P1: Recent titles include the following:\nL38@P1: Attractors for Semigroups and Evolution Equations\nL39@P1: OLGA A. LADYZHENSKAYA\nL40@P1: Fourier Analysis\nL41@P1: T. W. KÖRNER\nL42@P1: Transcendental Number Theory\nL43@P1: ALAN BAKER\nL44@P1: An Introduction to Symbolic Dynamics and Coding (Second Edition)\nL45@P1: DOUGLAS LIND & BRIAN MARCUS\nL46@P1: Reversibility and Stochastic Networks\nL47@P1: F. P. KELLY\nL48@P1: The Geometry of Moduli Spaces of Sheaves (Second Edition)\nL49@P1: DANIEL HUYBRECHTS & MANFRED LEHN\nL50@P1: Smooth Compactifications of Locally Symmetric Varieties (Second Edition)\nL51@P1: AVNER ASH, DAVID MUMFORD, MICHAEL RAPOPORT &\nL52@P1: YUNG-SHENG TAI\nL53@P1: Markov Chains and Stochastic Stability (Second Edition)\nL54@P1-2: SEAN MEYN & RICHARD L. TWEEDIECambridge University Press & Assessment\nL55@P2: 978-1-009-23005-6 — Fourier Analysis\nL56@P2: T. W. Körner , Foreword by Terence Tao\nL57@P2: Frontmatter\nL58@P2: More Information\nL59@P2: © in this web service Cambridge University Press & Assessment www.cambridge.org\nL60@P2: FOUR IER ANALYS IS\nL61@P2: T. W. KÖRNER\nL62@P2: University of Cambridge\nL63@P2: With a Foreword by\nL64@P2: TERENCE TAO\nL65@P2-3: University of California, Los AngelesCambridge University Press & Assessment\nL66@P3: 978-1-009-23005-6 — Fourier Analysis\nL67@P3: T. W. Körner , Foreword by Terence Tao\nL68@P3: Frontmatter\nL69@P3: More Information\nL70@P3: © in this web service Cambridge University Press & Assessment www.cambridge.org\nL71@P3: University Printing House, Cambridge CB2 8BS, United Kingdom\nL72@P3: One Liberty Plaza, 20th Floor, New York, NY 10006, USA\nL73@P3: 477 Williamstown Road, Port Melbourne, VIC 3207, Australia\nL74@P3: 314–321, 3rd Floor, Plot 3, Splendor Forum, Jasola District Centre, New Delhi – 110025, India\nL75@P3: 103 Penang Road, #05–06/07, Visioncrest Commercial, Singapore 238467\nL76@P3: Cambridge University Press is part of the University of Cambridge.\nL77@P3: It furthers the University’s mission by disseminating knowledge in the pursuit of\nL78@P3: education, learning, and research at the highest international levels of excellence.\nL79@P3: www.cambridge.org\nL80@P3: Information on this title: www.cambridge.org/9781009230056\nL81@P3: DOI: 10.1017/9781009230063\nL82@P3: © Cambridge University Press 1988\nL83@P3: This publication is in copyright. Subject to statutory exception\nL84@P3: and to the provisions of relevant collective licensing agreements,\nL85@P3: no reproduction of any part may take place without the written\nL86@P3: permission of Cambridge University Press.\nL87@P3: First published 1988\nL88@P3: First paperback edition (with corrections) 1989\nL89@P3: Reprinted with Foreword 2022\nL90@P3: A catalogue record for this publication is available from the British Library.\nL91@P3: ISBN 978-1-009-23005-6 Paperback\nL92@P3: Cambridge University Press has no responsibility for the persistence or accuracy\nL93@P3: of URLs for external or third-party internet websites referred to in this publication\nL94@P3: and does not guarantee that any content on such websites is, or will remain,\nL95@P3-4: accurate or appropriate.Cambridge University Press & Assessment\nL96@P4: 978-1-009-23005-6 — Fourier Analysis\nL97@P4: T. W. Körner , Foreword by Terence Tao\nL98@P4: Frontmatter\nL99@P4: More Information\nL100@P4: © in this web service Cambridge University Press & Assessment www.cambridge.org\nL101@P4: . . . mathematical ideas originate in empirics, although the genealogy is sometimes\nL102@P4: long and obscure. But, once they are so conceived, the subject begins to live a\nL103@P4: peculiar life of its own and is better compared to a creative one, governed by\nL104@P4: almost entirely aesthetical motivations, than to anything else and, in particular,\nL105@P4: to an empirical science. There is, however, a further point which, I believe, needs\nL106@P4: stressing. As a mathematical discipline travels far from its empirical source, or\nL107@P4: still more, if it is a second and third generation only indirectly inspired by ideas\nL108@P4: coming from ‘reality’, it is beset with very grave dangers. It becomes more and\nL109@P4: more purely aestheticising, more and more purely 1’art pour 1’art. This need not\nL110@P4: be bad, if the field is surrounded by correlated subjects, which still have closer\nL111@P4: empirical connections, or if the discipline is under the influence of men with an\nL112@P4: exceptionally well-developed taste. But there is a grave danger that the subject will\nL113@P4: develop along the line of least resistance, that the stream, so far from its source,\nL114@P4: will separate into a multitude of insignificant branches, and that the discipline\nL115@P4: will become a disorganised mass of details and complexities. In other words, at\nL116@P4: a great distance from its empirical source, or after much ‘abstract’ inbreeding, a\nL117@P4: mathematical subject is in danger of degeneration.\nL118@P4: von Neumann (from the first paper in his collected works)\nL119@P4: Some calculus tricks are quite easy. Some are enormously difficult. The fools who\nL120@P4: write the text books of advanced mathematics – and they are mostly clever fools\nL121@P4: – seldom take the trouble to show you how easy the easy calculations are. On the\nL122@P4: contrary, they seem to desire to impress you with their tremendous cleverness by\nL123@P4: going about it in the most difficult way.\nL124@P4: Being myself a remarkably stupid fellow, I have had to unteach myself the difficul\u0002ties, and now beg to present to my fellow fools the parts that are not hard. Master\nL125@P4: these thoroughly, and the rest will follow. What one fool can do, another can.\nL126@P4: (from Calculus Made Easy by Sylvanus P. Thompson)\nL127@P4: ‘Now,’ Herbie says, ‘wait a minute. A story goes with it.’\nL128@P4-5: (from A Story Goes With It by Damon Runyon)Cambridge University Press & Assessment\nL129@P5: 978-1-009-23005-6 — Fourier Analysis\nL130@P5: T. W. Körner , Foreword by Terence Tao\nL131@P5: Frontmatter\nL132@P5: More Information\nL133@P5-6: © in this web service Cambridge University Press & Assessment www.cambridge.orgCambridge University Press & Assessment\nL134@P6: 978-1-009-23005-6 — Fourier Analysis\nL135@P6: T. W. Körner , Foreword by Terence Tao\nL136@P6: Frontmatter\nL137@P6: More Information\nL138@P6: © in this web service Cambridge University Press & Assessment www.cambridge.org\nL139@P6: Contents\nL140@P6: Foreword by Terence Tao page xi\nL141@P6: Preface xiii\nL142@P6: Part I Fourier Series 1\nL143@P6: 1 Introduction 3\nL144@P6: 2 Proof of Fejer’s theorem 6 ´\nL145@P6: 3 Weyl’s equidistribution theorem 11\nL146@P6: 4 The Weierstrass polynomial approximation theorem 15\nL147@P6: 5 A second proof of Weierstrass’s theorem 19\nL148@P6: 6 Hausdorff’s moment problem 21\nL149@P6: 7 The importance of linearity 24\nL150@P6: 8 Compass and tides 28\nL151@P6: 9 The simplest convergence theorem 32\nL