Appendix G: Are share prices in Brownian motion? 581\nL284@P9-10: Index 585Cambridge University Press & Assessment\nL285@P10: 978-1-009-23005-6 — Fourier Analysis\nL286@P10: T. W. Körner , Foreword by Terence Tao\nL287@P10: Frontmatter\nL288@P10: More Information\nL289@P10: © in this web service Cambridge University Press & Assessment www.cambridge.org\nL290@P10: Foreword\nL291@P10: terence tao\nL292@P10: Mathematical monographs tend to fall, broadly speaking, into two categories. On\nL293@P10: one hand, one has the undergraduate texts, in which the basics of some subject are\nL294@P10: covered extensively and systematically, with many examples and exercises for the\nL295@P10: student to practice core techniques and concepts. On the other hand, one has the\nL296@P10: graduate texts, where the author assumes that the student is mathematically mature\nL297@P10: enough to recognise and fill in routine arguments and standard calculations, and is\nL298@P10: now ready to proceed to the cutting edge of the field.\nL299@P10: This book is a rare example of one that stakes a middle ground: it does not com\u0002prehensively introduce the fundamental definitions and results in Fourier analysis,\nL300@P10: but instead provides a sampler of bite-sized (and largely stand-alone) examples of\nL301@P10: how the themes of the subject (in particular, that of decomposing a general function\nL302@P10: into simple oscillating modes, and then reconstituting these modes back together to\nL303@P10: reconstruct the original function) arise naturally in both mathematical and practical\nL304@P10: contexts.\nL305@P10: One feature of this book that is particularly valuable for beginning students\nL306@P10: is that standard arguments in analysis that would be very quickly breezed through\nL307@P10: in more advanced texts (using phrases such as ‘by a routine scaling argument’,\nL308@P10: ‘by linearity we may assume without loss of generality that’, and so forth) are\nL309@P10: instead carefully worked through by the author. I would encourage such students\nL310@P10: to study and internalise these arguments when they are presented in this text,\nL311@P10: as they will certainly encounter them many more times in their studies and their\nL312@P10: research.\nL313@P10: The text does not need to be read in a linear fashion; I myself spent many pleasant\nL314@P10: hours as a graduate student browsing through whatever topics in the book took my\nL315@P10: fancy, whether it was reading about the fascinating history of the transatlantic cable,\nL316@P10-11: xiCambridge University Press & Assessment\nL317@P11: 978-1-009-23005-6 — Fourier Analysis\nL318@P11: T. W. Körner , Foreword by Terence Tao\nL319@P11: Frontmatter\nL320@P11: More Information\nL321@P11: © in this web service Cambridge University Press & Assessment www.cambridge.org\nL322@P11: xii Foreword\nL323@P11: the application of Fourier-analytic ideas to locate primes in arithmetic progressions,\nL324@P11: or using Fourier-analytic solutions to the heat equation to estimate the age of the\nL325@P11: Earth. I hope you find this book as enjoyable to dip into as I have.\nL326@P11-12: Department of Mathematics, UCLA, Los Angeles CA 90095-1555Cambridge University Press & Assessment\nL327@P12: 978-1-009-23005-6 — Fourier Analysis\nL328@P12: T. W. Körner , Foreword by Terence Tao\nL329@P12: Frontmatter\nL330@P12: More Information\nL331@P12: © in this web service Cambridge University Press & Assessment www.cambridge.org\nL332@P12: Preface\nL333@P12: This book is meant neither as a drill book for the successful nor as a lifebelt for the\nL334@P12: unsuccessful student. Rather, it is intended as a shop window for some of the ideas,\nL335@P12: techniques and elegant results of Fourier analysis.\nL336@P12: I have tried to write a series of interlinked essays accessible to a student with\nL337@P12: a good general background in mathematics such as an undergraduate at a British\nL338@P12: university is supposed to have after two years of study. If the reader has not covered\nL339@P12: the relevant topic, say contour integration or probability, then she can usually omit,\nL340@P12: or better, skim through any chapters which involve this topic without impairing her\nL341@P12: ability to cope with subsequent chapters.\nL342@P12: It is a consequence of the plan of this book that nothing is done in great depth\nL343@P12: or generality. If the reader wants to acquire facility with the Laplace transform or\nL344@P12: to study the L\nL345@P12: 2\nL346@P12: convergence of the Fourier series of an L\nL347@P12: 2\nL348@P12: function she must look\nL349@P12: elsewhere. It is very much easier to acquire a skill or to generalise a theorem when\nL350@P12: one is under the pressure of immediate necessity than when one is told that such a\nL351@P12: skill or generalisation might just possibly come in useful some day.\nL352@P12: Another consequence is that, although anything specifically presented as a proof\nL353@P12: or statement of a result is intended to meet the pure mathematician’s criteria for\nL354@P12: accuracy, the rigour of the accompanying discussion will vary according to the\nL355@P12: subject discussed. (Compare Chapter 3, Chapter 8 and Chapter 14.) For this I make\nL356@P12: no apology. ‘It is the mark of the educated mind to use for each subject the degree\nL357@P12: of exactness which it admits’ (Aristotle).\nL358@P12: I must however apologise for a major, though perhaps unavoidable, fault in this\nL359@P12: book. The historical remarks which I make in connection with certain problems\nL360@P12: are brief and, if only for that reason, paint only a small part of a very complicated\nL361@P12: picture. Moreover, a glance at the average history of mathematics shows that math\u0002ematicians are remarkably incompetent historians. I make no claim to superiority\nL362@P12: and can only advise that the reader consults the original sources before accepting\nL363@P12: the truth of any historical sketch drawn in this book.\nL364@P12-13: xiiiCambridge University Press & Assessment\nL365@P13: 978-1-009-23005-6 — Fourier Analysis\nL366@P13: T. W. Körner , Foreword by Terence Tao\nL367@P13: Frontmatter\nL368@P13: More Information\nL369@P13: © in this web service Cambridge University Press & Assessment www.cambridge.org\nL370@P13: xiv Preface\nL371@P13: Any textbook owes more to the books and lectures of others than to the nominal\nL372@P13: author. At one stage I had a list of over 25 names of unwitting contributors to this\nL373@P13: one. However, a long list prompts more interest in its omissions than its inclusions\nL374@P13: so I shall simply record my immense debt to the inspiring lectures of G. Friedlander,\nL375@P13: J.P. Kahane, H.P. Swinnerton-Dyer and H. Shapiro.\nL376@P13: This book would never have seen the light of day without the labours of sev\u0002eral generations of Cambridge typists. I should like to thank in particular Robyn\nL377@P13: Bringan, Debbie McCleland and Betty Sharples. It would have contained many\nL378@P13: more great and small mathematical errors without the careful scrutiny of Jonathan\nL379@P13: Partington, Richard Hildich, Chris Budd and an anonymous referee.\nL380@P13: I close the preface by dedicating this book to my parents with love and respect.\nL381@P13: T.W. Korner ¨\n"
